  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The emission reductions are estimated using a counterfactual simulation model rather than observed from physical, real-time vehicle control deployments.",
      "what": "The study establishes a counterfactual wave-smoothing benchmark using a quadratic program with fixed boundary conditions and a maximum-gap constraint of 0.1 miles to reconstruct smooth vehicle trajectories. Using the MOVES model on nine weeks of weekday peak-period empirical data from the I-24 MOTION testbed, the researchers estimated average CO2 reductions of 9.80% to 14.08% across lanes for passenger cars, alongside reductions of up to 25.70% in CO, 29.76% in HC, and 36.03% in NOx.",
      "why": "It quantifies the realistic, large-scale emission reduction potential of smoothing freeway traffic waves under practical gap constraints, revealing that targeting trucks yields roughly double the emission benefits of targeting passenger cars."
    }
